/* book exam traps: Per metre versus total: putting the total L of a 20 m cable into √(L/C) gives an answer wrong by √20. Convert to per-metre values first. | Using free-space c for vp. On a line vp = 1/√(LC) is fixed by the dielectric and is normally below c. | Writing Z0 = √(L/C) when R and G are not zero. That formula is the lossless line only; the general Z0 is complex. | Dropping the minus sign in the backward current, I(z) = (V⁺/Z0)e−γz − (V⁻/Z0)eγz. With a plus sign the backward wave would carry its current the wrong way, and every Γ and Zin built on it comes out wrong. | Thinking Z0 can be measured with an ohmmeter on an open cable. The meter reads the DC open-circuit resistance, which is nearly infinite, not Z0. */
